(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F1: the 2022 National Survey counted 14.4M hunters (about 6% of people 16+), used a new \"push to web\" method, and USFWS says 2022 must not be compared with any earlier survey.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F1: on a comparable method, 14.1M hunters 16+ in 1991, 13.7M (6%) in 2011, 11.5M in 2016. The 2016 report itself says its method matches 1991-2011, so 1991-2016 is one comparable run.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F1: in 2016, 9.2M big-game hunters and 8.1M deer hunters (71% of all hunters). The table's text extraction drops the decimal point (\"8 1\" = 8.1).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U36: hunters 16+ were 14.063M (1991), 13.975M (1996), 13.034M (2001), 12.510M (2006); the participation rate fell from 7.4% to 5.5%; hunting kept pace with population until 1991. The numbers hold. The finder's framing that deer \"peaked near 2000 just as hunters started declining\" is loose: FWS dates the turn to 1991, so hunters had already been falling for a decade by 2000. The deer-peak half belongs to pass C.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U68: the 2011 method matched 1991-2006; from 2001 to 2011 hunters rose 5% and big game 6%; surveys before 1991 are not comparable, so a 1955-to-2022 or 1985-to-2022 line crosses method breaks. The pre-1991 break is confirmed from the 2016 report, not from the 1991 report itself, which I did not open.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U49: 2022 counted ages 6-15 through a screener (1.8M young hunters); Outdoor Life's subtraction gives 12.6M. That figure is the outlet's own arithmetic, not a USFWS series. Trap: the same article calls 14.4M \"a 25 percent increase\" over 2016, which is exactly the comparison USFWS bars.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U98: 2022 big-game hunters 11.5M. Seen only in RMEF's relay (it says \"deer and elk\"); I did not open the 2022 USFWS report, so I can't confirm there is no deer-only count. RMEF repeats the no-comparison warning.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F2: in 2016 about 60% of hunters were 45+ (22% aged 45-54, 24% aged 55-64, 14% aged 65+). The dossier's chart-order worry is settled: the 2016 full report states 24% for 55-64 in words and 2.5M (22% of 11.5M) for 45-54. The 14% for 65+ is read from the chart, in the same order. \"About 60%\" is our sum.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F2: Wisconsin's DNR ties part of its decline to baby boomers reaching 65.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F3 Michigan: 604,088 deer-license buyers in 2024 (+1.6% on 2023), down 31% since 1995 (1.3% a year on average); 532,926 actually hunted deer; youth 74,070, down 19% since 1995. Now confirmed at the michigan.gov PDF (fetched with curl -k), replacing the radio-station relay. The DNR slides label the 604,088 both \"people purchased a license\" and \"TOTAL DEER HUNTERS\", so say \"license buyers\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F3 Wisconsin: male deer hunters aged 12-80 fell from 567,721 to 472,873 (-17%) and female hunters rose from 51,634 to 66,661 (+29%), 2005-2022, a net loss of about 80,000 (-13%). These are individual hunters, not licenses sold.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F3 Wisconsin: participation fell in every age group 2005-2024; 2024 rose slightly; early 2025 data look similar to 2024. Archery rose 2005-2022.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F3 Wisconsin to 2040 (Mohr et al.): males -27 to -40% in all four scenarios. CORRECTED on the female range: the dossier says \"roughly 0 to +44%\", but the DNR says a 5-44% increase. The error came from the engine finding's wording. Total hunters still fall, which holds.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U70: 790,044 Wisconsin deer privileges sold through Nov 30, 2025 (-0.12%), 550,611 of them gun-only. The source is a DNR release reprinted on Urban Milwaukee. Privileges are not hunters: never set this beside the A11 individual counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U70: Wisconsin gun license sales peaked at about 700,000 in 1990, held there until 2001, and are now as low as the mid-1970s. Holds, but the line is in the DNR's March 2026 research article, not the Dec 2025 release the finder cited.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U69: the 2007 Winkler/Huck/Warnke draft: gun deer hunters fell from 645,047 (2000) to 600,787 (2007); the cohort-component model gives a LOW projection of about 428,000 male gun hunters by 2030, against about 550,000 in 2007. The same paper's share model projected slight increases. The 2013 paper's figures (55,304; 88,552) were not opened, so don't air them. This study is superseded by Mohr et al. (A13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F4 Colorado: CPW wildlife revenue FY2024-25 was $220M, with licenses, passes, fees and permits 68% and federal grants 17%. Whole agency: $378M, 58% from licenses. The dossier's dollar breakdown ($150.7M, $37.2M, $350K, 0.16% General Fund) is NOT on this page, and the wildlife pie has no General Fund line. Use the percentages only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F4 Wisconsin: in 2023-24 about 92.7% of Fish and Wildlife Account revenue came from license, permit and stamp fees (excluding a $25M forestry transfer); gun deer licenses were 17.6%. Resident gun deer licenses are down 16.5% since 2013-14. Source is LFB paper #530.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F4 Michigan: the DNR budget is $543.2M (67% state restricted, 19% federal, 13% GF/GP); the Game and Fish Protection Account is $80.5M; hunting and fishing licenses brought in about $68.4M in FY2024-25. The House Fiscal Agency also ties falling license numbers directly to cuts in game and fish programmes, a useful primary for the \"who pays\" thread.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F4: Pittman-Robertson FY2026 final apportionments were Michigan $23,914,949, Wisconsin $23,485,531, Colorado $22,930,115, and $842,403,264 in total (basic + hunter ed + enhanced hunter ed).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U99: the AFWA survey (FY2019 revenue, all 50 states, $3.7B dedicated revenue) found 69% from licenses 43%, Pittman-Robertson 18% and Dingell-Johnson 8%. The page is Boone and Crockett's write-up by the survey's own researchers (Booher and Millspaugh), not an AFWA document.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW (closes the F6 trap that \"how apportionments are computed is NOT sourced\"): by statute, half of each state's Pittman-Robertson share follows its land area and half follows its count of paid hunting-license holders, with a 0.5% floor and a 5% cap. So fewer license buyers does shrink a state's share of the federal pot, even when the tax itself is paid mostly by non-hunters.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F5: Wisconsin's account general revenues are estimated at $14.9M a year below general spending in 2025-27 (DNR had said $15.9M); DNR says it has used more Pittman-Robertson and Dingell-Johnson money to cover it; Wisconsin's Pittman-Robertson apportionment was $25.7M in FFY2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U86/F5: the Legislative Audit Bureau found the account spent $126.2M in FY2023-24, supported by $92.9M state and $33.3M federal money, and would have been about $10M short without the $25M forestry transfer. Checked only at WPR: the LAB report 25-15 URL I tried returned 404. On air, say \"a state audit found\", attributed to the audit through WPR.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F5 restatement (\"the record shows a shortfall but NOT that hunter decline caused it\") holds, and the primary names a cause: resident fees have been frozen since 2005 while prices rose 64%. Gun deer license sales are also down (A18), so decline contributes, but the official account of the gap is fees plus inflation. Wisconsin's $24 resident deer license is the cheapest among its neighbours (Michigan $31).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F5 trap: \"a $15M-a-year forestry transfer in 2025-27\" (from a search summary). WPR says lawmakers are moving $30M from the forestry account under the current budget. LFB's options were $16.126M each year or a one-time $29.84M. $15M a year over two years is close to $30M, but the enacted figure was not seen at a primary. Say \"about $30 million\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F4 trap \"the $25M came from the forestry account, NOT the general fund\": literally true, but by law the forestry account is itself filled by an annual general-fund transfer pegged to property value ($166.0M in 2025-26). Don't air \"no tax money\", because the backfill is general tax money one step removed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U35: \"The US Fish and Wildlife Service itself ... states that the Pittman-Robertson tax base has shifted.\" CORRECTED on attribution: the Feb 2025 addendum carries a disclaimer that its conclusions are the author's (Jerry Leonard, FWS economist), not official FWS positions. It cites the shift to Duda (2021) and the 74.2% to Southwick (2021), calling it \"one recent study\". The 47M target-shooter count is the survey's own. The error came from the finder's wording. Say \"a 2025 report for the Fish and Wildlife Service, citing an industry estimate\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U58: Southwick (an industry firm, from proprietary data) puts hunting-related purchases at 22.5% of taxable firearm and ammunition sales, \"just over a fifth\" of Pittman-Robertson. The finder's \"archery sales could not be split\" is not on the page. Trap: 22.5% hunting and 74.2% non-hunting (A28) don't sum to 100 and may come from different Southwick cuts, so use one framing: \"about three-quarters comes from non-hunting purchases\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U106/F6: Duda et al. 2022 (Human Dimensions of Wildlife) says non-hunters now contribute a disproportionately greater share of Pittman-Robertson. This was checked at the abstract (the \"wording from an abstract\" sample), which holds. It gives no percentage. The dossier's \"USGS and Colorado State authors\" is not shown on the page, which lists names only, so don't air affiliations.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U64: Smith & Molde (2014) contend that about 95% of federal, 88% of non-profit and 94% of total wildlife funding comes from non-hunters. They exclude state agencies and assign the hunter share by a flat 4.6% population assumption. A further weakness: they count the whole US Forest Service ($9.8B) and National Park System ($3.65B) budgets as wildlife funding. \"Contend\" is the authors' own word. Don't air this as a finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F7 Missouri: the conservation sales tax (\"one-eighth of one cent\") brought in $174.8M in FY2025, 61.8% of $282.9M revenue, with permits 16.2% and federal 16.2%; no state general revenue. The FY2027 request is 61.3% / 17.7% / 14.5%. The department says recreation spending already generates more sales tax than its own tax collects.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F7 Arkansas: Amendment 75 (1996) levies one-eighth of one percent on taxable sales; Game and Fish gets 45%. Light detail: it passed with 50.5% (405,216 to 396,932). The source is the Encyclopedia of Arkansas (a reputable reference, not the constitution itself).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F7 Minnesota: the Legacy Amendment raises the sales tax three-eighths of one percent from July 1, 2009 to June 30, 2034, with 33% to the Outdoor Heritage Fund.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F7: the Recovering America's Wildlife Act passed the House 231-190 on June 14, 2022 (H.R. 2773, 117th Congress) and was not enacted. The 118th bill (H.R. 10390, Dec 12, 2024) was referred to committee. The money ramps from $850M (FY2025) to $1.3B a year from FY2028, plus $97.5M a year for Tribes, transferred from the Treasury's general fund. So \"about $1.3B a year\" is the full rate, and its funding is general tax money.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F7: \"no 119th-Congress bill\" for the Recovering America's Wildlife Act. Not established either way: one web search found none, the GovTrack text search was too broad to read, and the congress.gov API was rate-limited. On air, say \"it has never passed the Senate\" and nothing about the current Congress.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F18/U67: one Maine columnist paid a $155 processing fee on just over 51 lb of boneless venison from a 103-lb buck, about $3/lb, against $7/lb for 90%-lean ground beef (his range was $4.80-$7). The BLS average for 100% ground beef was $6.792/lb in Sept 2026 ($6.323 in Sept 2025). Processing only: no license, gear, travel or time.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F18: Wisconsin raised processor reimbursement to $125 a deer (was $100), or $135 with CWD testing (was $110), in an Aug 20, 2026 release. Since 2000, over 100,000 deer and over 4M lb.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a38"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U47: Wisconsin publishes cumulative totals only: Nov 21, 2024, over 98,000 deer and nearly 4M lb since 2000. The \"about 2,000 deer between releases\" is arithmetic on rounded \"over\" figures, so don't air it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a39"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F18: Hunters Feeding Michigan (a DNR programme) processed a record 140,000 lb in FY2025 (about 560,000 servings). Seen only through American Hunter (NRA journal, Oct 28, 2025) relaying the DNR, not the DNR release.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a40"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW: Michigan DNR's 2024 harvest survey asked about donation. 21% of hunters reported donating a deer; 55% of donors did it because they had extra venison and 26% to help manage the herd; 69% of hunters who never donated say they use all their venison.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a41"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U97: Michigan Sportsmen Against Hunger says it has donated 1M+ lb since 1991; funding got harder after it stopped receiving the donations collected at license purchase \"last year\" (Oct 2025 article). This is the charity's own claim. The 2014 DNR bulletin ($1/lb, about 30,000 lb) was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a42"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U7 (Wisconsin wildlife damage programme, 2023-24): $823,600 in claims, $411,900 on abatement, $1,776,700 to counties; payments don't vary by animal; the $2 surcharge raised $2,053,700. CORRECTED on the bonus permits: the finder counted all $1.84M as damage money, but $667,200 of it went to CWD, and only $1,172,300 stayed with damage programmes. Total damage revenue was $3.23M. The \"hunters fund it\" point holds. The error is the finder's compression.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a43"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U34 (Virginia; herd half is C's): the deer estimate rose from about 1.1M (pre-2014 season) to 1.3M in 2023 and 2024; objectives are met in 37 of 97 counties; 177,541 deer license holders in 2025/26 against more than 343,000 in 1986; sales down 19% in 10 years and 34% in 25; licensed deer hunters are about 2% of Virginians. The \"abundance must decrease in 56 counties\" figure is not in the page text (probably in the map image), so don't air it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a44"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U38 (pass C's ground): US deer peaked around 2000. The source is a letter by G. Kent Webb disputing Rochlin et al. (2022), with a published reply from them. It cites Webb's own 2018 estimate (ResearchGate) and Flather et al. (2013, USFS, 29 states). Direction is plausible, but this is one author's contested estimate: don't air a national deer trend from it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a45"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U52 (pass C's ground): Bridge Michigan, dated Oct 21, 2024 (the finder had no date). DNR's Fedewa says hunters could kill 35-40% of the deer each fall \"without putting a dent\" in numbers; Michigan hunters kill 1.3 bucks per doe, the highest ratio in the Great Lakes; stability needs tens of thousands more does a year; liberalised rules brought no big response.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a46"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U56 (pass C's ground): onX relaying NDA's 2026 Deer Report says 11 states have more deer than in 2019 (Louisiana +100%) and 5 fewer (including Wyoming and Arkansas); 5 of 6 states with early 2025-26 data showed harvest declines; deer hunters were 65% of all hunters in 2024, down from 78% in 2021.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a47"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U63 (pass C's ground): NDA's 2025 Deer Report (2023-24 season) puts buck harvest at 3,086,182, only the second time above 3M in 25 years (2020: 3,044,735); 3% more bucks than antlerless, the largest gap since 1999; doe harvest was up to 31% above buck harvest in 2000-2015; antlerless harvest \"is not keeping pace\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a48"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U42 (pass B's ground): Purdue (Indiana): 15 treatment and 36 control roads among 51 covering 949 km; collisions down a predicted 21.12% along 618 km; about 69.6 prevented 2018-2022; mechanism unconfirmed (fewer deer, or deer avoiding hunters).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a49"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U53 (pass C's ground): Fairfax (White Buffalo) sterilized 52 does; recruitment fell from 1.29 to 0.19 fawns per female; deer down about 39% (66 to 40), 10.5 to 6.3 per sq mi; 16 does died (10 in car crashes, 4 shot by hunters) and 10 were unlocated. County staff say over 90% of females must be treated, and surgical sterilization costs about $1,000 a deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a50"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U90 (pass C's ground): Solon, Ohio's USDA sharpshooting cost the city $183,353 for 300 deer, $611.18 a deer all-in, or about $408 a deer on outside sharpshooting and processing costs alone.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a51"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U0 / F8, Michigan 10-year deer-crash trend. Crashes 46,870 (2016) to 58,324 (2024) is right (+24.4%), and the 2020/2021/2022 points are right. WRONG: \"deaths stayed in a band of about 10-19 a year\". The primaries give 14 (2016), 14 (2018), 12 (2019), 5 (2020), 10 (2021), 11 (2022), 19 (2023), 14 (2024), 13 (2025). The band is 5-19. The gaps the finder flagged are now filled from the primaries: 2018 = 53,464 crashes, 1,335 injured; 2019 = 55,531, 1,429 injured; 2023 = 58,806, 1,725 injured, 19 killed. Deer crashes also went from 15.0% to 20.2% of all Michigan crashes. Error source: engine finding (the finder could not open the PDFs and relied on relays). The 2014 value (45,690) was not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U50, Michigan 2024 and 2025. In 2024: 58,324 crashes, 1,816 injured, 14 killed (7 motorcyclists). The 2025 figures are now confirmed at the primary (the official 2025 PDF does exist; the finder had a radio relay only): 58,271 crashes, 1,724 injured, 13 killed, 8 of them motorcyclists. Fatal deer crashes peaked in June in both years, while crashes peak in November.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U1, Wisconsin 2025 and 2023 (WisDOT fact sheets). 2025: 17,795 crashes, 6 killed (4 motorcyclists), 720 injured. 2023: 16,153 crashes, 14 killed (12 motorcyclists), 585 injured. Trap: WisDOT's own narrative bullets are inconsistent. The 2023 sheet says the death and injury \"peak\" was 2017 (6 killed), and the 2025 sheet calls 2025 (6 killed) a \"peak\", yet 2023 had 14 deaths. Use the counts, not the \"peak\" wording.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U1, Wisconsin 2024 values (17,432 crashes, 9 killed, 707 injured). The 2024 WisDOT fact sheet URL returned a non-PDF error page and no other copy was found. Not verified, so do not air 2024. The 2023 and 2025 endpoints in B3 carry the trend (+10%).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U2, IIHS/FARS deaths in collisions with ALL animals: 189, 189, 212, 190, 186, 202, 164, 188, 234, 210 for 2015-2024 (read from the saved table rows), and the trend has \"leveled off\". FARS does not record the species. An old nine-state review found 77% of animals in fatal crashes were deer. Animal-crash deaths peak in July-September, not in the autumn rut. Not checked here: the \"about 40,000 US road deaths\" comparator.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U3 / F8, State Farm, July 2025-June 2026: more than 1.6M animal-vehicle collision claims and $1.9B paid. These are ALL animals. The release's footnote says \"animal\" claims include rodents chewing vehicle wiring, so not every claim is even a collision.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U4, the 2026 release gives NO deer count and NO average claim cost. Deer are only \"by far the most frequently struck animal\". The $1.9B / 1.6M (about $1,190) figure is our arithmetic across all animals. The only deer count is the prior year's: more than 1.1M of about 1.7M claims.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U5, 2025-26 state odds: WV 1 in 46, MT 1 in 54, WI 1 in 62, PA 1 in 64, MI 1 in 68. National odds went from 1 in 139 to 1 in 145. Pennsylvania had the most claims, about 141,000.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U74, the 2025 release (July 2024-June 2025): Michigan about 126,000 claims, MI 1 in 61, WI 1 in 58. No dollar figure appears anywhere in the release text. Extra: October-December was about 650,000 claims, or 41%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U73, Colorado on State Farm's 2025-26 map is 1 in 253, \"Low Risk\". Ten jurisdictions have longer odds (WA 305, NV 838, CA 430, AZ 682, NM 292, CT 289, AK 580, HI 852, FL 543, DC 635), so Colorado is about 41st of 51. The rank is OUR count from the map image, not a State Farm ranking.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U72, FHWA 2008 Report to Congress: about 300,000 reported large-animal collisions a year, 26,000 injuries, 200 deaths; 1-2M including unreported. This is large animals, not deer only, and it is 2008. The VTRC 2017 \"1.3 million\" insurance figure in U72 was NOT checked (see UNFINISHED).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U42 / F9, Indiana (Purdue + Indiana DNR, Science of the Total Environment 2024), read via Purdue's release, which quotes the paper. 15 treatment vs 36 control roadways (51 roadways, 949 km); collisions fell a predicted 21.12% along 618 km; 69.6 prevented 2018-2022; mechanism unknown (fewer deer, or deer avoiding hunters). Extra: up to $653,756 in damages saved, and the zone licences raised $206,268.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U94, Oak Ridge Reservation, TN. Now read at the primary abstract (DOAJ API record; the paper is Muller et al., Wildlife Society Bulletin 2014) and the related UT master's thesis abstract. 4,637 collisions 1975-2008; peak of 273 in 1985, when harvest began; then a decline (about 100 a year by 2008, per the thesis). Prior-year harvest was positively related to collisions. NEW and useful: no hunt was held in 2001 (cancelled for security after 9/11), and collisions rose for the next two years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U40, Clarke County, VA (McShea et al., Human-Wildlife Interactions): 228 segments, autumn 2005-06. Deer density and harvest were not correlated with collision locations; traffic, road type and housing were. The rut made no marked difference.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U41, Bavaria (roe deer): about 74,000 collisions in 2006 and 2009; collision risk positively correlated with harvest numbers; \"simply increasing harvest numbers is not appropriate\". Trap: the authors treat harvest as another density index, so this is harvest following density, not harvest causing crashes. They also still list deer management as one option.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U85, Penn State Deer-Forest blog (a researcher's post, not peer-reviewed): 22 GPS-collared deer, Nov 14-Dec 6 2021. No abrupt movement change at the rifle opener. Males declined steadily after the rut; females rose slightly. It measures movement, NOT collisions. \"Doesn't support a hunting-season spike\" is our inference.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U20, Raynor, Grainger and Parker, PNAS 2021: wolf entry cut deer-vehicle collisions 23.7% in the average county (robustness 17.9-27.3%), about 38 a year. Sample is 63 counties (29 wolf, 34 non-wolf), 1988-2010.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U21, decomposition: 17.4% is behaviour and 6.3% is population (about 10 fewer collisions); 73% of the effect is behavioural.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U78, the valuation: $9,960 per collision, more than $375,000 per county per year, $10.9M across 29 counties, versus $3.1M in compensation 1985-2019 ($174K a year recently), a ratio of 63:1, with compensation likely an underestimate. Trap: $9,960 is a national willingness-to-pay cost that includes \"pain and lost quality of life\", not an insurance payout.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW, central to the episode: the PNAS authors explicitly compare wolves with hunters. They say hunters' effect on deer behaviour is compressed into a few weeks, while wolves act all year. This is the authors' interpretation, not a separately tested result.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Published critique or replication of the PNAS wolf paper: one targeted search found no formal comment, letter or reply. Press coverage at the time carried outside experts' reactions, which were not saved or checked. Absence is not established, so do not say \"unchallenged\". The paper's own limits: one state; preferred window ends 2010; it is a county-level panel, not an experiment.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U54, cougars (Gilbert et al., Conservation Letters 2017; DOAJ abstract plus UW release). Confirmed: 22%, 21,400 injuries, 155 deaths, $2.13B over 30 years in the East; $1.1M a year in South Dakota; the 3-of-4 compensatory-mortality assumption; range kept to large forests. NOT checked: \"the authors did not model hunter recreation or Lyme\" (full paper not opened). The UW release's annual figures are 5 deaths, 680 injuries and $50M a year.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U9, Kilpatrick, LaBonte and Stafford 2014 (the sample of a claim worded from an abstract). The numbers match the abstract, but the abstract's own verb is causal (\"resulted in\") for a single-community before/after with resident-reported cases. Air it as \"coincided with\", which is what the design supports.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U10, Monhegan Island, Maine (Rand et al. 2004): every deer removed 1996-99; ticks rose first (28/h, 75% infected), then collapsed (0.67/h by fall 2003) while the reference island's ticks kept rising. Extra: by 1996, 13% of year-round residents had had Lyme.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U11, Jordan, Schulze and Jahn 2007, suburban NJ: a 46.7% herd cut (2,899 to 1,540) had no apparent effect on questing ticks and no clear trend in Lyme incidence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U100, Kugeler et al. 2016 (CDC-authored review), on the Lyme evidence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U76 (in F13), Martin et al. 2023, eight eastern national parks. Confirmed: +49% nymphs per 1 SD (about 16.2 deer/km2) of lagged deer density; deer density not significantly tied to infection prevalence; only Rock Creek significant after reduction (58 to 14). NOT checked: \"lower at four of five parks\" (the saved text says only that the other four contrasts were not significant), and the Fischhoff 2019 and Kilpatrick 2017 parts of U76 (not opened).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U66, Lyme part only: Humane World's plan does say CDC and health authorities don't recommend hunting for Lyme \"because it hasn't been shown to work\". Its framing is stronger than the CDC-authored review (B26: \"insufficient evidence ... except in specific ecologic circumstances\"), and its line that killing deer \"doesn't reduce\" ticks is contradicted by Monhegan (B24). Attribute it as advocacy. The fertility and rebound parts belong to pass C.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U6, Wisconsin's wildlife damage program 2023: $1,363,891 appraised across 203 claims; deer damaged 2,432 of over 3,300 acres. This is a voluntary enrolment program (1,010 enrolled), not a statewide damage estimate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U8, NASS 2001: $944M all wildlife; $619M field crops; about 12,000 operators by telephone. The \"58% deer\" wording is slightly off. It is deer's share of the species named by operations reporting field-crop damage (respondents could name several; shares adjusted to sum to 100), not \"a share of operations reporting deer damage\". NASS itself says it should not be turned into dollars. \"No newer national edition\" was not tested here.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U75, Marquis 1981 (USFS NE-475), Allegheny Plateau. The $1,075/acre figure is the projected stumpage value lost AT STAND MATURITY (age 80): fenced $2,377 vs unfenced $1,102. The author calls it a crude estimate from a \"gross oversimplification\" equation. Spread over the rotation, it is about $13 per acre per year. 92% of fenced plots were satisfactorily stocked vs 38% of unfenced. The \"1981-dollar basis\" is implied by the date but not stated.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U88, hemlock (Alverson, Lea and Waller 2019, Can J For Res; abstract via Crossref). WRONG: \"grew about 5.2 times taller\". The abstract says protected cohorts \"shared a combined height 5.2 times greater\": a cohort total that folds in their higher survival, not per-seedling growth. Right: survival 59.3% vs 15.0% (about 4x); deer are the primary constraint; more than 2,000 seedlings at 59 sites over 20 years. Also: two First Nations reservations had lower deer density and better unprotected-seedling survival than three national forests. Error source: engine finding's paraphrase of the abstract. NOT checked: the Flambeau maple/ash \"no effect\" clause.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11. Wisconsin's 2025 post-hunt herd estimate is about 1.89M, the highest ever. Read in a local paper quoting the DNR deer-metrics page (the DNR page is an image). Antlerless harvest rose 5% year over year. It is a record HERD, not a record harvest.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11. Wisconsin 2025, all seasons combined: highest buck harvest since 2007 and highest total since 2012, with 339,481 registered by Jan 20 against 317,976 in 2024. Sourced to the DNR's acting deer program specialist speaking to the Natural Resources Board, as reported by Outdoor News; not a DNR release. Every season was above its five-year average except the nine-day gun season.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Wisconsin's 2025 nine-day gun season (preliminary, DNR Dec 2, 2025): 182,084 deer (86,068 antlered, 96,016 antlerless), down 0.8%, with antlered down 2.6% and antlerless up 0.9%. The season ran Nov 22-30.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11. \"A Michigan DNR specialist says 35-40% of the herd must be harvested each year just to hold steady.\" The figure is real, but it is a staff remark reported two ways, not a published model. Bridge quotes it as the share hunters could take \"without putting a dent\" in the herd (the annual surplus). Michigan Farm Bureau reports the DNR as saying \"at least 35%\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11. Michigan hunters take 1.3 bucks per doe, the highest ratio in the Great Lakes states. Officials estimate Michigan \"may\" have 2 million deer (an estimate in the press, not a DNR population model). Loosened antlerless rules got little response.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW, not in the dossier. In May 2026 Michigan's Natural Resources Commission voted a one-buck rule for the Lower Peninsula from March 1, 2027 and cut antlerless opportunities across almost all of it, against the DNR's own biologists. Michigan's 2024 harvest of about 300,000 is roughly half its 1990s level. Source is Michigan Farm Bureau, an interested party (dated May 15, 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11. Virginia: herd about 1.1M (pre-2014 season) rising to 1.3M (2023 and 2024); deer licences peaked above 343,000 in 1986 and fell to 177,541 in 2025/26; the state needs fewer deer in 56 of 97 counties. Note the two series cover different windows (herd 2014-2024, licences 1986-2025). Over the herd's own ten years, licence sales fell 19%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U39 / F11. \"National deer numbers peaked around 2000 (USFS and Webb).\" The source is a one-author letter (Webb 2022) attacking Rochlin et al. 2022, and the journal ran a reply from Rochlin. It rests on Webb's own 2018 estimate (posted on ResearchGate) and on Flather et al. 2013, a study for the USFS with a 29-state sample showing a peak in 2000 and only a modest decline to 2010. A defensible line, but a disputed one: \"appears to have peaked around 2000\", not a settled USFS finding.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U57. National Deer Association 2026 report, relayed by onX (the NDA report itself was not read): 11 states report more deer than in 2019 (Louisiana +100%) and 5 report fewer (including Wyoming and Arkansas); five of six early-reporting states had harvest declines in 2025-26; deer hunters were 65% of hunters in 2024, down from 78% in 2021; antlerless harvest topped 3.1M. The NDA's \"not keeping pace\" quote (F11) is not on this page and was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U13. Tensas River NWR model. WRONG: the dossier says the herd \"grew with no female harvest (lambda 1.15 observed)\". RIGHT: 1.15 is a PROJECTION for zero female harvest at observed fawn survival. The herd actually observed was growing at λ=1.06 under the current (about 10%) female harvest, and was projected to decline at 20% (0.98) and 30% (0.94). Adult female survival was the most elastic parameter in every scenario. Error source: finder's wording. The Penn State \"just under 20%\" commentary was not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U84. South Dakota (GFP 2025 report): statewide whitetail harvest fell from about 51,600 (2020) to 40,700 (2024), mainly from hemorrhagic disease and winter losses. Doe harvest was cut to about 16,000. The Black Hills whitetail objective is 70,000 (65,000-75,000). \"Substantially below objective\" sits in the mule deer sentence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U37. FWS trend report: hunting kept pace with population growth until 1991, then fell. Hunters 16+ (thousands): 14,063 (1991), 13,975 (1996), 13,034 (2001), 12,510 (2006); the participation rate fell from 7.4% to 5.5%. The \"1970-2000 deer boom\" half of the claim is NOT in this source (it is the finder's juxtaposition). Hunter counts also belong to pass A.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F11, the unhunted-herd case. Rock Creek Park (DC, no public hunting) peaked near 100 deer per square mile. NPS culling since 2013 brought it to about 19, against the 15-20 a healthy forest needs. George Reserve and Fire Island were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U15. Ann Arbor (White Buffalo, Year 4): city helicopter count 289 down to 137 (53%); about 13 deer per sq mi in Wards 1-2 and about 11 where sharpshooters had access; about 17 projected after fawning. Reductions: 47% South Study Area, 60% North Study Area, 58% Wards 1-2. TRAP: the North Study Area's \"60%\" is 15 deer down to 6. Deer were INCREASING in Wards 3-5, where little shooting happened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F12. Ann Arbor costs over four years: sterilization $159,815 for a 49% cut on 2.8 sq mi; sharpshooting $255,800 for a 58% cut on 8.3 sq mi. The contractor itself warns that \"sterilization costs 2-3x sharpshooting\" misleads, because shooting was not feasible in the sterilization zones.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U16 / U18. Michigan DNR report to the legislature (PA 390 of 2018), read from a Rhode Island newspaper's file mirror, not michigan.gov. Ann Arbor sterilization cost $1,572 per deer against a $1,185 average; \"not meant to be a widespread population management tool\"; 47% after four years; at most 115 culled (2018); not a replacement for recreational hunting at scale. The 29-56% range includes communities that also used lethal removal, and all its figures come from DeNicola & DeNicola 2021, the contractor's own paper. The same report also says hunting may shift from the primary method to one of many.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F12. Fairfax (the CITY of Fairfax, a low-density site, not the county): White Buffalo sterilized 52 does; deer fell about 39% (66 to 40, i.e. 10.5 to 6.3 per sq mi); half the does died or dispersed (10 hit by cars, 4 killed by hunters). County staff say over 90% of females must be treated, and put fertility control at $500 (PZP) to $1,000 (surgery) a deer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U19. Staten Island (NYC Parks releases): about 2,053 deer (Jan 2017) to about 1,616 (Jan 2021), a 21% drop, with fawn births down 60%. Earlier: 1,555 and 24% (2020); 15% and 77% fewer fawns (2019, a survey by White Buffalo \"verified by NYC Parks\"). Note the 2021 decline is SMALLER than 2020's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U92, part 1. Staten Island 2016: a $2M three-year sterilization study fast-tracked by emergency procurement, with White Buffalo as contractor. A 2014 aerial survey found 763 deer, up from 24 in 2008.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U92, part 2. \"About $4.1M spent by 2019; about $2,600 per animal for about 1,456 bucks (NY Post); a $2.5M extension in Sept 2019.\" Not opened at any source in this pass. Keep it off air, or attribute it to the NY Post as reported.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U17. Valley Forge (NPS with USDA Wildlife Services sharpshooters): 241 deer per sq mi (1,277 deer) in 2009 down to 44 per sq mi (234) in 2016, against an initial target of 31-35; 100 deer removed over five nights in winter 2015-16. The 2014-15 figures (195 deer over 14 nights, the 35-per-sq-mi projection) are not on this page and were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U91. Solon, Ohio, 2012, USDA sharpshooters: 300 deer (Jan 30 to Mar 19) for $183,353 in total, or $611.18 a deer. Counting outside sharpshooting and processing costs only, it was about $408 a deer (Patch, quoting the city's summary report).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F12. GonaCon-Deer label (EPA, 2021): a restricted-use pesticide for USDA Wildlife Services or state wildlife agency staff, or persons working under their authority; a doe is infertile for at least one year; the effect may wear off in the second year; meant to be used alongside other methods; female deer only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F12. GonaCon trial results: 88% then 47% effective (Maryland, 28 treated does) and 67% then 48% (New Jersey, 28 deer; White Buffalo co-ran it). These are CONFIRMED at the USDA factsheet. The Maryland site was fully fenced, and Wildlife Services sharpshooters thinned it BEFORE the contraceptive went on. The \"EPA-registered 2009\" date is NOT confirmed at a primary: this factsheet predates registration and only says USDA planned to submit data in early 2009.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U93 (Rutgers part). 8-foot high-tensile woven wire is \"the gold standard\", costs about $10 a foot to install (undated page), and gives more than 20 years of protection; only high-value crops justify it. The Penn State 2007 prices and the \"no fence excludes all deer\" line were not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Dossier summary: \"most of their results come from one contractor\". WRONG as stated. The STERILIZATION evidence is all White Buffalo (Ann Arbor, Fairfax, Staten Island, and the DeNicola & DeNicola 2021 review behind the Michigan DNR's 29-56%), and White Buffalo is a non-profit, not simply a vendor. The SHARPSHOOTING records are government shooters: USDA Wildlife Services at Valley Forge and Solon, NPS at Rock Creek, IDNR in Illinois. RIGHT: \"the sterilization results come almost entirely from one contractor; the culling records are the government's own.\" Error source: engine summary (over-generalised from F12's evidence line).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U23. USGS: CWD documented in free-ranging and/or captive cervids in 37 states as of June 2026 (ver. 4.0, August 2026). Wild and captive are mixed, so don't say \"wild deer in 37 states\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U24. Wisconsin DNR, Oct 22, 2026: first CWD-positive wild deer in Marinette County, near Peshtigo, a 3-year-old buck dispatched for signs of illness; the county's baiting and feeding ban runs three more years. The 2025 CWD-year table (April 2025 to March 2026) shows Marinette at 183 sampled, 183 analysed, 0 positive (my row parse), so this is a new detection after a clean year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U25 / F14. Wisconsin's 2025 seasons (DNR release Mar 4, 2026): 17,759 sampled, 2,006 positive; Southern Farmland Zone 7,656 samples at 22.7% (1,737); first positives in La Crosse and Clark. TRAP: 11.3% (2,006/17,759) is correct arithmetic but is NOT a statewide prevalence. Sampling is targeted, and 87% of positives came from one zone. The later 2025 CWD-year table shows 2,041 of 18,370.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U80. Wisconsin's cumulative county table (released through Oct 31, 2026). Dossier: about 16,612 positives of 353,868 analysed, 52 counties, and \"no totals row\". My parse of today's page: 73 counties sampled, 354,858 sampled, 354,535 analysed, 16,621 positive, 53 counties with at least one positive. The page DOES carry a \"Statewide Totals:\" row, left blank. Marinette shows 1 of 3,159 (matches). The live table drifts, so air \"about 16,600 positives from about 355,000 tests since 2002\" and call it our sum.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U83 / F14. Southwest Wisconsin CWD, Deer and Predator Study (Iowa, Dane and Grant counties): annual survival is 83% for uninfected vs 41% for infected females, and 69% vs 17% for males; populations decline once female prevalence passes about 29%. The DNR also says it does not expect herds to be wiped out. Cumulative county rates: Iowa 8.7% (4,737/54,707) and Richland 13.2% (2,935/22,212), my division.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U26 / F14. Michigan DNR, Feb 12, 2026: Gladwin became the 18th county; 376 positive wild deer of about 148,800 tested. Today the DNR locations page shows 389 positives in the same 18 counties (plus 3 of unknown county), 203 of them in Montcalm alone.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U30 baseline: \"120 positives in nine counties, more than 60,000 tested, July 2019\". Not found. The cited bulletin does not contain it, and the only 2019 DNR figure found is 65 positives from just over 20,000 tested in the 2019 seasons (Feb 2020). Don't air a 2019-to-2026 rise until the July 2019 release is found.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U32 / F16. Illinois FY2026 CWD report: adult prevalence in the 28-county endemic area is 10.4% (553/5,331), the highest in the programme's history; 662 positives from 13,225 statewide samples; the affected area is 29,501 sq mi; 3 new counties. IDNR has \"permanently suspended\" targeted sharpshooting and says the effect on long-term prevalence is unknown. Sharpshooters still removed 1,258 deer (73 positive) in winter 2026, so the suspension follows that winter. The report also says \"fall 2026\" for FY2026 harvests, likely a typo for fall 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U33 / F16. Manjerovic et al. 2014 (Prev Vet Med 113:139-45; Illinois Natural History Survey authors): no state difference while both culled (2003-07). After Wisconsin stopped in 2007, it averaged 3.09 ± 1.13% over the next five years, rising 0.63 points a year, while Illinois stayed flat. The university release gives Illinois about 1% for 2002-2012 and Wisconsin rising to \"about 5 percent\". Illinois hunter harvest rose from 147,830 (2001) to 181,451 (2012). No published critique found (one search). TRAP: the 1% is statewide tested deer, while today's 10.4% is the endemic-area adult average, so they are not the same metric.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg hunt runner-up 1. Wisconsin DNR EHD page, updated Oct 29, 2026: confirmed in 4 counties, suspected in 4 more; county estimates (Washington \"just over 1,300\", Waukesha around 630, and others) sum to about 2,385 (our sum, and these are reports, not confirmed deaths). The DNR says herds \"will quickly rebound\" and outbreaks end at the first hard frost; DATCP found EHD in Waukesha dairy cattle in Sept 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"\"Largest EHD die-off in state history\". The DNR page itself never says \"largest\", but its own history table backs it: before 2026 the biggest year was 321 suspected reports (2012) and 308 (2019), against about 2,400 now. Spectrum News attributes \"largest\" and \"about 300\" to the DNR. Air it as \"more than seven times any earlier Wisconsin outbreak on the DNR's own records\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Dossier summary: alternatives \"cost about $400-2,600 a deer\". At primaries the range runs from $408 (Solon, outside costs only; $611 all-in) through $1,000 (Fairfax staff estimate for surgery) to $1,185-1,572 (the contractor's own sterilization figures). The $2,600 top end is the NY Post's Staten Island figure and is unchecked (C20). Air \"roughly $400 to $1,600 a deer at the sources we checked\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F17 / U45 / U46: the Blue Book says Amendment 83 makes no immediate change to state law and that its effect depends on how courts read it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U45: carve-outs (nongame, endangered, federally illegal species), \"traditional methods\", and the state keeps power to regulate for science, safety or future opportunity. Note the Blue Book paraphrases; the measure's own wording is \"sound scientific wildlife conservation and management\" and \"preserve the future of hunting and fishing opportunities for all species statewide\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg / F17: the measure declares hunting and fishing THE preferred means and needs 55%. Script note: Colorado says \"the preferred means\"; North Carolina's constitution says \"a preferred means\". Initiative #302, received April 2, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U102: fiscal impact: no significant near-term spending change, possible higher CPW legal costs, amount cannot be estimated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U43 / peg \"Why now\": the For and Against arguments as printed. The \"24 other states\" and \"$115 million, one-third\" figures sit inside the Argument For, i.e. the proponents' case printed by the Council, not its neutral analysis. (Reconciling $115M with CPW's own budget is pass A's.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F17 trap / U60 said the Blue Book's \"24 other states\" is a count conflict with Ballotpedia/NCSL's \"24 in total\". WRONG: there is no conflict. 24 states have the right today and Colorado is not one of them (NCSL's list of 24 omits Colorado), so Colorado would be the 25th and \"24 other states\" is correct. KUNC uses the same wording. Error came from the engine finding. Say \"24 states have it; Colorado would be the 25th\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U60: Vermont since 1777, 23 others since 1996; 25 ballot measures, 24 approved (Alabama twice); Arizona 2010 the only rejection. (NCSL says \"20 of those approved via the voters\", a different tally from Ballotpedia's 23; use Ballotpedia's table.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F17 says the other states \"passed with 67-89%\". WRONG: the floor is 57%. North Carolina 2018 passed with 57% and Virginia 2000 with 60%; Florida 2024 was 67%. Right range: 57% to 89%. Error came from the engine finding (U96 had said \"most others\" 75-89%; the finding compressed it).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U96: Alabama 2014 789,777 (79.84%); Florida 2024 6,941,307 (67%); Wisconsin 2003 668,459 to 146,182 (82%); Tennessee 87%, Mississippi 88%, Utah 75%. (Florida's \"all 67 counties\" not checked.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW: Colorado's is a citizen initiative; every one of the 25 earlier right-to-hunt ballot measures in Ballotpedia's table is marked LRCA (legislatively referred). So Amendment 83 appears to be the first one put on a ballot by petition. This is our reading of the table, not a sentence either source states.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U103 / F17: \"preferred means\" is in Ballotpedia's model text; NCSL names Alabama (2014), North Carolina (2018) and Utah (2020) as adding it. Neither source says ONLY those three, Wisconsin's 2003 text was not read, and \"post-2012 addition\" is inference. Say \"at least three states, including North Carolina\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U44: Oates v. Berger, N.C. Court of Appeals COA24-559, 16 April 2025, unanimous: the right to hunt is not fundamental, chiefly because the amendment reserves power to the General Assembly; Sunday-hunting limits get rational-basis review and stand. The opinion quotes \"preferred means\" only inside the amendment text and does not analyse it. Read at a case-law mirror (full opinion text).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U44: the N.C. Supreme Court declined the case in December 2025 (it granted the defendants' motion to dismiss the appeal). Read in Carolina Journal, not the court's order.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F17 says Oates is \"the one court ruling found\". WRONG: Ballotpedia's own movement page lists three more: Tennessee chancery court 2015 (paddlefish limits upheld), Virginia circuit court 2005 (called the right \"fundamental\" but held sporting clays is not hunting), Wisconsin Supreme Court 2004 (amendment allows any reasonable regulation). Every case found upheld the regulation. Courts split on \"fundamental\" (Virginia trial court yes, N.C. appeals court no). Error came from the finder's search scope.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U59: money to the Oct 19 TRACER deadline: Yes $2.61M+ raised; opposition committees $224,190; IOTR $1.61M+ (about 60%); CSF $350,000; Farrell-Roeh $150,000; Hunter Action Fund $150,000; Damaske $100,000; SCI $55,000; $1.65M to The Synapse Group for signatures; the largest No committee about $168,435 incl. $100,000 from the Center for a Humane Economy and $30,000 from the Sierra Club's Denver chapter; AWA $411,341 and Humane World $151,982 in ads. Secondary (Vail Daily, Oct 22; Summit Daily ran it Oct 21), citing SOS filings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U61: opponents spent $142,784; Pacelle's two groups put $510,000+ into advertising against it; outside groups $563,000+.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW, framing: hunters are on both sides. The biggest No committee is a hunters-and-anglers group led by a co-founder of the Theodore Roosevelt Conservation Partnership.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW: signatures: 124,238 needed; certified Aug 20 with 145,000+ valid. The article gives the submitted count two ways (184,000 in the text, 180,000 in a photo caption); say \"over 180,000\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg hunt IDX3: RMEF and Hunt 2 Conserve committed $125,000 to support the measure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U104 said the Animal Wellness Action release named FIVE commissioners and claimed a majority oppose. WRONG on both: it names six (\"at least six\"), and the saved release contains no majority claim. Right: the Commission takes no stance; six commissioners oppose personally; chair Jay Tutchton (\"Tuchton\" in the release) said \"lawyer bonanza\" (quoted from KUNC, Oct 20). Advocacy source. Error came from the finder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U65: Humane World for Animals opposes; its case is \"already legal\", undefined \"traditional methods\" (trapping, hounding, baiting), and \"preferred means\". The page never uses the word \"deer\". Project Coyote and the 40-vet letter were NOT checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW, advocacy figures in the same document, not checked at USFWS: \"10 times more wildlife watchers than hunters\" and \"defeated similar efforts in eight states in 2026\". Attribute or drop.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U107: PETA says hunting is not needed, creates overpopulation conditions, and the alternatives are reducing fertility and preserving predators; it names no method. (Absence of a PETA statement on Amendment 83 not tested.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U95: Colorado has voted both ways: Prop 114 (wolves, 2020) passed 50.91%; Prop 127 (lion/bobcat/lynx hunting ban, 2024) failed 45.26% to 54.74%. Wikipedia (secondary).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg: Amendment 83 is the only right-to-hunt measure on any 2026 ballot (Ballotpedia, secondary).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F15 / U101: Thompson, Samuel and Van Deelen 2008 (JWM 72(2):416-421): no feeding method or ration cut transmission risk; a feeding ban is warranted.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F15 / U26: Huang et al. 2025 (JWM 89(4) e70000), northern Mississippi CWD zone (~30% prevalence): prions on all deer AND raccoon feeders by week 12; deer-to-deer contact highest at feeders; 4 of 19 feeders positive where CWD was newly found. Not the Michigan State/DNR study (see D31).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F15 / U80: Miller et al. 2003: feeding associated with bovine TB in NE Michigan deer, 1995-97, 59% of 389 took part, Poisson regression; risk rose with deer fed and grain quantity; a feeding ban is a valid policy. A different disease from CWD.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F15 / U27: Cosgrove, O'Brien and Ramsey 2018 model: bans helped lower bTB but about 25% non-compliance and legal feed sales impede eradication; attraction to food piles had the biggest effect; DMU 452 ban since 1999.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW, same paper, cuts against \"the ban cut the harvest\": it says baiting in general does not increase harvest, and that participation and antlerless harvest in the banned zone matched the rest of the state. It also prices the feed market ($15M to Michigan farmers in 1995, the crop side of HB 4445).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U105 cites IPR for both the 2019 \"no data\" quote and \"a 2025 MSU and DNR study\". WRONG source: the IPR story is dated March 28, 2019 and the quote is Sen. VanderWall's about a 2019 Senate bill, not HB 4445. The 2025 MSU/DNR study is reported by Bridge Michigan (Feb 4, 2026), which says deer \"concentrate more densely\" at bait sites (density, not a contact measure). The study itself was not read. Error came from the finder merging two sources.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW: Michigan's ban timeline. bTB zone (DMU 452) since 1999; a Lower Peninsula ban from 2008 after CWD, lifted for most of the LP in June 2011; the current LP-wide ban took effect at the end of January 2019 (Jan 31). Bridge's \"since 2018\" conflicts; probably the year the rule was adopted (not verified). Say \"since 2019\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The \"harvest fell from ~600,000 before the ban to under 300,000 since\" claim (source: WGRD radio, Dec 2025, unsourced). WRONG in three ways. (1) The DNR's own survey estimate since the ban is about 350,000-375,000, not under 300,000: 347,008 in 2023 and 373,300 in 2024. (2) \"Just under 300,000\" is the count of harvest REPORTS hunters filed (280,304 in 2024), which the DNR says runs 25% below the true harvest; it is a different measure. (3) The ~600,000 is the 1998 record (Alpena News 2021, secondary; the DNR's own 1998 figure was not obtained), two decades before the 2019 ban, and the DNR said 2019 license sales fell only as fast as earlier years. The big drop happened before the ban. Error: source wording (radio station) mixing a survey estimate with a report count.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d33"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"NEW: Michigan 2024 (DNR deck, June 2025): 604,088 license buyers (+1.6%), 532,926 actually hunted, 373,300 deer taken; the voluntary survey is being retired for mandatory reporting.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d34"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"F15 / U30: hunters did not fall faster after the 2019 ban. The DNR bulletin (Feb 27, 2020) confirms license sales -3% in 2019, in line with earlier years, smallest drop in the CWD core area. NOT in that bulletin: the 2018 figures (554,331 hunters, 361,000 deer), the 2019 harvest (~364,000), and the director's line about the 2008-2011 ban. Those remain unverified at primary (DNR survey PDFs unreachable this session).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d35"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U28 / peg: HB 4445 (Rep. Wortz, introduced 5/6/2025) passed the House 66-38 (6 not voting) on 2/4/2026 with immediate effect; referred to Senate Natural Resources and Agriculture 2/11/2026; no later action. Its text allows baiting during open deer seasons, leaves FEEDING to a Commission order, and has no CWD exception or volume limit.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d36"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U55: House committee heard testimony Dec 10, 2025 and reported it without amendment Dec 17; no Senate action since. DNR stance per Bridge: open to lifting the ban but wants the Natural Resources Commission, not lawmakers, to do it. The Farm Bureau's \"DNR opposes\" version was not checked. The peg's \"Senate adjourned until Nov 3\" was not re-checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d37"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"U23 / peg: Wisconsin DNR, Oct 22, 2026: first CWD-positive wild deer in Marinette County near Peshtigo, a 3-year-old buck put down for signs of illness; the county's EXISTING baiting/feeding ban is extended three years (the detection lengthens a ban already in place, it does not create one); neighbours unchanged; state law sets 3 years for the county and 2 for adjoining counties within 10 miles; the release gives the prion mechanism. (0 of 183 in 2025 is pass C's county table.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d38"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg \"Why now\": State Farm (Sept 15, 2026): 1.6M+ animal-collision claims, $1.9B paid, national odds 1 in 145, Michigan 1 in 68. All animals, not deer only. 47 days old: script material, not the peg.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d39"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg \"Why now\": Wisconsin DNR EHD page updated Oct 29: confirmed in 4 counties, suspected in 4 more; county estimates sum to 2,385 (our addition, reports not confirmed deaths); the DNR expects a quick rebound.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d40"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Peg / humour: Wisconsin's Nov 21 openers are \"Crow\" and \"Gun Deer\", side by side in the DNR's November list. (Michigan's Nov 15 opener was verified by the main session, not re-checked here.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d41"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CHURN, national (MEASURED). Southwick Associates for NSSF, 2015 summary deck. It used 10 years of complete license records from 12 states and was funded by a USFWS Multistate Conservation grant. 22% of resident hunters will not renew next year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CHURN, national (MEASURED). The typical hunter bought a license 2.8 years out of 5, and only 13% bought one in each of the last 10 years. Both are slide-image text (p.9 and p.10 of the PDF) and do not appear in the extracted text. These are all hunting licenses, not deer only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CHURN, regional (MEASURED). The Midwest has the most loyal license buyers: 47% buy every year for 5 years, against 41% in the Northeast, 29% in the West and 20% in the Southeast. Slide-image text, p.18.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"SAY vs DO (STATED against MEASURED, one study). 80% of new hunters say they will hunt in each of the next 5 years; 20% actually do. Slide-image text, p.16-17. The survey half was run by Responsive Management for the Southwick/NSSF study. This is the single best \"what people say versus what the records show\" fact in the record.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WHO LAPSES (MEASURED). Younger hunters and women are less likely to renew. Rural hunters renew at 74%, suburban at 71% and urban at 66%, which fits the \"moving to cities\" story at the license level.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WHAT PROMPTS A HUNT (STATED, Southwick/RM for NSSF). The top reason new hunters give for going in a given year is \"an invitation from a friend\". \"Moved to a home near huntable lands\" is also in their top three. This is the mentor/partner effect from the other side.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CHURN, current range (MEASURED, via a 2025 AFWA Multistate Conservation Grant description; the grantee is not shown on the page). Churn \"held between 21% to 27% annually for the past five years\" on the CAHSS-era license dashboard, and the pandemic did not notably reduce it. The page says the dashboard is \"grant-funded\" but gives no year-by-year figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"THE REASONS ARE POORLY KNOWN (the field's own admission). The same 2025 grant says states have \"a limited understanding\" of why hunters choose other recreation, and that the newest national study of what competes with hunting is 14 years old (NSSF 2011). This anchors the honest answer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CHURN after the COVID bump (MEASURED, Council to Advance Hunting and the Shooting Sports, 2022 release; Southwick worked on it). National hunting license sales fell 1.9% in 2021 (resident sales -4.0%). On the 20-state dashboard, new recruits fell 9% and churn rose 1%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WISCONSIN churn (MEASURED; DNR license data relayed by Patrick Durkin in American Hunter, June 2015, so secondary). Deer-hunting churn was 28% in 2014. 39% of deer hunters (393,566) bought a deer license in all seven seasons 2005-2011. About 28.5% of the roster changed each year, \"about double what was long assumed\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"SKIPPING ISN'T QUITTING (MEASURED, NSSF/Southwick 2009, relayed by American Hunter). The pool of people who hunt is about 50% larger than the licensed count in any one year: 21.8M hunted at least once in five years. 78% re-bought the next year, but only 35% bought five straight years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"SKIPPERS ARE CASUALS (STATED by an agency official, not measured). Wisconsin's Learn to Hunt coordinator says half of gun licenses sell the week before the season, and casual hunters drop a year over a choir recital, work or the weather. Usable as colour, labelled as his description.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WISCONSIN, all ages fell, not just boomers (MEASURED; DNR study, Mohr et al., relayed in Durkin's column of Mar 23, 2024). The DNR's deer researcher says the decline is across all ages and is not only boomers ageing out. Male gun-only hunters fell about 37%, from about 350,000 (2005) to about 220,000 (2022). Builds on A9/A12.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Durkin's headline loss (\"lost 65,000 deer hunters … roughly 672,000 to about 607,000\", most since 2014) uses a different base from the DNR's own article (A11: about 80,000 lost, 13%, residents aged 12-80). Air A11, not the column. The column's \"78.5% since 2014\" has no primary here.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"COHORT, not age alone (MEASURED/modelled; peer-reviewed, Erickson, Reeling & Lee, Animals 2019). Participation is \"largely invariant to age until the hunter becomes ~65\". The ongoing decline is more likely a cohort effect: boomers hunted more than people born after 1980 and are not being replaced. This is their reading of Winkler & Warnke 2013.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Winkler & Warnke 2013 (Population and Environment), checked at the abstract. A16 said its figures were \"not opened\". The abstract projects Wisconsin male deer hunters down more than 10% (55,304) in 10 years and another 18% (88,552) from 2020 to 2030. MEASURED projection, superseded by Mohr et al. (A13). Don't air both.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"RECRUITMENT PIPELINE, Wisconsin (MEASURED; DNR's 2022 R3 biennial report to the Legislature). Gun-deer license sales fell 10.4% from 2016 to 2021 (about 52,000 fewer residents). Hunter-education certifications were 31,103 in 2013 and 27,598 in 2021. In 2020 new gun-deer buyers rose over 36% while total gun-deer sales still fell 1.3%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WISCONSIN DNR lapsed-hunter study, 2010-11 (STATED: 7 focus groups plus mail surveys of lapsed and annual hunters, by DNR's Robert Holsman). Known only through notes of his 2015 Midwest Deer and Wild Turkey Study Group talk, so it is secondary and has no sample size. Drivers named: poor deer encounters, frustration with deer rules (earn-a-buck, extra seasons), land access and license cost. Earlier studies had found \"lack of time and lack of places to hunt\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WHO LAPSES in Wisconsin (STATED survey, same Holsman study). Lapsed hunters were more likely to hunt deer only, rely on public land and hunt in smaller groups. Focus groups saw public land as overcrowded. The talk suggested that access and deer availability set a \"carrying capacity of hunters\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"WISCONSIN 2019 survey of deer hunters who skipped that year (STATED; DNR's own survey). \"Life and family\" was among the most cited reasons, with answers about not getting time off. The source is the DNR's March 2026 research article (pass A's file). It gives no percentage or sample size, so say \"one of the most cited\", never a share.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The Wisconsin DNR's own list of causes is a list, not a measurement: urbanization, generational shifts and falling youth participation, life and family constraints, and other hobbies. The DNR says \"these factors are not exhaustive\". STATED by the agency.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Michigan DNR's stated explanation (STATED by the agency in its Feb 2020 bulletin): ageing generations plus younger people less likely to hunt \"due to societal changes\". No Michigan lapsed-hunter survey was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"STATED reasons with a sample size (Responsive Management for the Rhode Island DEM, 2022; outside the Midwest, used only as an agency example). Among license holders whose hunting decreased (n=738, multiple answers), the top reasons were free time/family or work obligations (43%), then access and quality land. For hunters who started in the last 5 years (n=319), lack of free time was the only obstacle above the midpoint (5.4/10), followed by \"not knowing where to go\" (4.8). Only the 43% is safe to air: the chart's other bar values extract out of order.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"ACCESS, where deer are killed (MEASURED by agencies, compiled by the National Deer Association's Deer Report and relayed by bowhunting.com, Jan 2023, 2021-22 season). 88% of the harvest is on private land; Midwest 91%; 27 of 47 states run a private-land access program.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"CROWDING (MEASURED density, same NDA relay). Wisconsin has 11.4 deer hunters per square mile, third in the nation; Michigan has 9.5, fifth; Pennsylvania leads with 14.4. Read beside T1-19, Wisconsin's crowding complaint has a number under it. Hunters per square mile is not the same as hunters per public acre.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Wisconsin's harvest was 87% on private land in 2021 despite more than 7M public acres. Seen only in search snippets of an Outdoor Life article that now returns a 404. The NDA state table was not opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"COST of a hunting year (MEASURED spending, STATED by respondents; USFWS 2022 National Survey, new \"push to web\" method, so not comparable with earlier surveys, per A1). The average hunter spent $857 on trips and $550 on equipment in 2022. That covers all hunting nationally, not deer only. Beside the $24 and $31 deer licenses (A25), the license is a small part of the bill.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"license price drove them out\" (our inference from rows already CONFIRMED, labelled as such). Wisconsin's resident fees have been frozen since 2005 while prices rose 64% (A25), so the real price of a deer license has fallen, and hunters still left (A11). Cost appears in survey lists (T1-18) but cannot be the main measured driver of the Wisconsin decline.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"CWD scares hunters off\", Wisconsin (MEASURED; peer-reviewed model on 1966-2015 permit sales, Erickson, Reeling & Lee, Animals 2019). Permits peaked at 952,711 in 2000. They fell 102,710 (over 10%) after CWD was found in 2002 and archery recovered. The model puts CWD's lasting effect at 5.4% of demand, about 48,000 permits a year, and the firearm drop was more permanent. So CWD caused a real one-time shock, but it is a minor share of the long decline.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The same paper says its model \"does not provide insight\" into the secular decline since 2008. That is the authors' own limit on blaming CWD.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"CWD scares hunters off\", Michigan (MEASURED, DNR bulletin Feb 2020). In 2019 hunter numbers fell least in the CWD core area. This counts against a CWD flight in Michigan. It is one year and the DNR gives no figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Michigan's 2018 CWD hunter survey (Frawley, Pomeranz, Isenhoff; DNR Report 3667) reportedly found 12% (31,215) would stop deer hunting and 28% were unsure. Seen only in a search snippet of a DNR slide deck. The question wording (conditional on what?) is unknown and both PDFs return 404. STATED intention; T1-4 shows how far intentions drift from behaviour. Do not air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"the baiting ban drove Michigan hunters away\" (re-check of D35/F15). The DNR's own bulletin says 2019 license sales fell about 3%, \"mirrored past years\", despite predictions. Still APPROXIMATE: the DNR's 2018 and 2019 survey reports (hunter counts) remain unreachable (404, Wayback blocked), so the comparison with \"past years\" is the DNR's word, not a series I could check. Caveat (our arithmetic from A10): about 3% is above the 1995-2024 average of 1.3% a year, so \"mirrored past years\" must mean recent years. Michigan's big fall (D33) came well before 2019.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-33"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Michigan 2019 deer hunters = 540,174 (a search summary attached this to 2019). CORRECTED as far as search snippets go: the 540,174 belongs to the 2020 survey abstract. No primary was saved (404), so the 2019 count itself is unresolved. Don't use 540,174 for 2019.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-34"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"anti-hunting culture\" (STATED attitudes; Responsive Management for the Outdoor Stewards of Conservation Foundation, spring 2023, 2,021 completes). 77% approve of legal hunting and 69% approve of deer hunting. The trend chart reads 73% (1995) rising to 81% (2021), then 77% (2023), read from the chart labels in order. So approval ROSE through the decades hunters fell. The softening is recent: approval for most reasons was at its lowest in 2023.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-35"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MYTH \"just make more deer / easier rules and they'll come back\" (Michigan, A46). Liberalised antlerless rules brought \"no big response\". Wisconsin's columnist (opinion, labelled) notes the steepest losses came after the 2014 \"new era\" changes and after online registration. Pair it with T1-10: in Wisconsin, roughly one in four buyers turns over every year whatever the rules.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-36"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED. The strongest R3 study found: Gassett, Williams & Chase (Wildlife Management Institute, 2022, a USFWS/AFWA Multistate Conservation Grant) matched 60,000 R3-event attendees in eight states to lookalikes drawn from 13 million license buyers. The eight are Connecticut, Georgia, Iowa, Massachusetts, Missouri, New Mexico, North Dakota and Tennessee: neither Michigan nor Wisconsin, and the events mix hunting and fishing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (WMI). Result: attendees kept buying licenses more regularly and bought more of them, and the authors say part of that is BECAUSE of the event, not only because keen people sign up.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (WMI). Size of the effect: attendees \"churn\" (skip a year) about 11% less than lookalikes in North Dakota up to 36% less in Connecticut. TRAP: the summary says \"all states\", but in New Mexico attendees and lookalikes churned at about the same rate. Say \"in seven of eight states\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (WMI). The catch: 27% to 93% of attendees had already bought a license before the event, so most of the gain is keeping people who were already half in, not adding new ones.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (WMI). Youth events gave the smallest lift and adults 25-50 the biggest; basic introductory events beat advanced ones. The authors tell agencies to put adult programs ahead of youth programs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED by von Furstenberg et al. (Wildlife Society Bulletin 47, Sept 2023; 20 authors). \"Hunting 101\" workshops for college students with no hunting experience, 16 states: 314 took part; 12-18 months later 34% REPORTED having hunted. Self-report, and only 133 answered the follow-up, so the 34% is of responders, not of all 314. No license check.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED, but only by the organiser. National Deer Association (then QDMA) Field to Fork: \"80 percent hunted on their own within the first year\". The page is from Oct 2018, after the third year of ONE program (Athens, Georgia) that guided 15 adults that year. No method, sample or follow-up length is given. Attribute it (\"the organisers say\") or drop it.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED by Holsman & Kool (UW-Stevens Point, 2011), as summarised in the Wisconsin DNR's own 2020 report to the Legislature. Wisconsin's Learn to Hunt (since 1996) was mostly reaching kids who were already from hunting families and already in hunter safety; the evaluators said the programme needed goals and outcomes it could be measured against.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (same study, interview arm, UWSP thesis). The record-keeping was too thin to follow graduates: 187 past participants were identified, and 12 were interviewed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. As of the 2020 report, Wisconsin Learn to Hunt participants had a median age of about 13, and the DNR was only then wiring Learn to Hunt records into the license system so it could see whether graduates buy licenses. So no license-based outcome existed for the state's oldest programme.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED by the Wisconsin DNR from its own license records (no comparison group). Hunt for Food, the adult programme created in 2012 in response to the Learn to Hunt evaluation: most participants had never hunted. 59.3% bought a Wisconsin hunting license the same year, 48.3% one year later, 41.2% two years later. The best Wisconsin number in the record: about 4 in 10 brand-new adults still buying two years on. It is small: DNR staff say they are near capacity.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. Who Hunt for Food reaches: in 2021-22 about 54% men and 46% women, median age 38 for men and 35 for women. Learn to Hunt was 77% male over the same period.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (uptake counted, retention not). Wisconsin's mentored hunting law (since Sept 1, 2009) lets anyone hunt within arm's reach of a mentor before taking hunter education. Mentored-license buyers rose from 12,553 (2016) to 18,286 (2021); in 2021, 5,536 were adults. The report gives no figure for how many mentees later bought a regular license. Small internal inconsistency: the text says 12,553 for 2016 while the chart shows 12,364.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (the cost, not the effect). Legislative Fiscal Bureau paper #530: in license year 2024-25 Wisconsin sold 181,028 new-buyer licenses and approvals (fishing included) at $3.98M in revenue forgone, and 42,852 mentored licenses. All discounts together came to 981,816 items. The LFB frames discounts as recruitment that \"may\" work and floats general-fund money to cover them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. Wisconsin's first-time price today: $5 for many resident hunting seasons if you haven't held that license in 10 years. There is also a \"recruiter\" reward: name the person who got you into it, and three namings in a year earn them a discount.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED, informally, by columnist Patrick Durkin (Jan 25, 2016) using DNR sales figures. The $5 deal (from 2012) sold 18,973 first-time gun-deer and 16,119 first-time archery licenses by the 2014 opener, yet gun-license sales fell 4% that year to 609,779, the lowest since 1976. A 2012 DNR survey found few first-time adult buyers even knew they'd had a discount. The DNR survey itself was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED, informally (same column). The neatest test of the discount: a Winkler (UW) and Warnke (DNR) forecast made BEFORE the price cut predicted 2014 deer-license sales to within 1,000. The line didn't bend. One columnist's reading, but the comparison is his, from the researchers' numbers.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (trend, no attribution). A decade into the discount, Wisconsin's resident first-time license buyers fell about 4.5% from FY16 to FY21 (all first-time types counted together).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MEASURED (Wisconsin DNR), not a programme: the biggest jump in new deer hunters in the record came from the pandemic. New gun-deer buyers rose over 36% in 2020 while total gun-deer sales still fell 1.3%. Over 2016-2021, with every programme above running, gun-deer license sales fell 10.4% (about 52,000).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (law), outcome UNFOUND. Michigan's apprentice license (Public Act effective July 10, 2006) lets someone with no hunting experience hunt with a mentor for up to two license years. The law ordered the DNR to report its effect on recruiting new hunters by Oct 1, 2008. That report was not found in this pass, so no Michigan apprentice outcome is on the record.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-20"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. Michigan's Mentored Youth Hunting Program (law in force Sept 1, 2011; licenses from 2012): $7.50 license for kids under 10 hunting with a mentor 21 or older. The required DNR report is about REVENUE every four years, not whether kids keep hunting. Source is Deer & Deer Hunting (secondary) describing the bills.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-21"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (uptake). Michigan mentored-youth deer-season licenses: 10,824 (2022), 11,650 (2023), 12,437 (2024), up 6.8% in the last year. Uptake is growing; no follow-on purchase figure was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-22"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED, loosely (DNR spokesman via Great Lakes Echo, Feb 16, 2015). Michigan's March 2014 overhaul (an $11 base license, more than 200 license types cut to about 40) RAISED prices. The DNR expected about 7% fewer buyers and lost slightly fewer, while revenue rose $8M in eight months. Useful the other way round from discounts: a price RISE barely moved buyers. No DNR table was seen.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-23"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MEASURED (Michigan DNR harvest survey), NOT attributed by the DNR. This builds on A46 (Oct 2024: past loosening brought \"no big response\"). For the 2024 season Michigan opened its early and late antlerless seasons to public land and added a $5 antlerless license. That year antlerless licenses rose 7.9%, antlerless harvest rose 18% and buck harvest was unchanged, while license buyers rose only 1.6%. So more does were taken by roughly the same hunters. Mixed by season: late antlerless fell 24%, late urban rose 258%. One year; say \"the year after\", not \"because of\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-24"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. Michigan's Hunting Access Program pays farmers to let the public hunt. It dates from 1977, started in southern Michigan where 97% of land is private, and was expanded with the federal VPA-HIP grant. The DNR page gives no current acreage and no hunter-use or recruitment figure (search snippets ranged 19,000-25,000 acres; not used).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-25"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED for MONEY, not for hunters (Southwick Associates, June 2012). The national VPA-HIP evaluation was meant to run three years but was cut to first-year estimates when Congress eliminated funding late in 2011. Users' spending beat the federal dollars in each state, but Michigan's first year returned just under $1 per $1 of all funds spent. Nothing in it measures whether access made or kept hunters.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-26"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED. Wisconsin's VPA (federally funded through USDA NRCS) plus its turkey-access programme covered over 38,000 acres of private land in 2020 after a $1.9M grant. Leases run $5-25 an acre a year. Today the DNR is not enrolling new properties. No use or recruitment outcome is published on either page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-27"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MEASURED (DNR sales relayed by Durkin, 2016). Wisconsin opened crossbows to all archers in 2014, and archery licenses hit a record 275,417 that year while gun licenses fell. Secondary relay of a DNR count.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-28"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED by the Wisconsin DNR (\"Wisconsin's Evaluation of Crossbow Use and Season Structure\", Oct 2019, 141 pp., a Natural Resources Board directive). Crossbows mostly gave EXISTING hunters an extra option rather than pulling gun hunters away: gun-only hunters were more likely to add a crossbow than give up the gun. Of hunters holding both gun and bow licenses, only 0.7% a year dropped both for crossbow only. Only 2% of lapsed gun hunters took up a crossbow.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-29"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (Wisconsin DNR 2019). Where crossbows DID keep hunters: people aged 40-65 who couldn't or wouldn't use a vertical bow. License revenue rose about $50,000 a year in the five years after, against about $450,000 a year of losses before. But the DNR warns the 2014 deer-rule overhaul confounds any credit to crossbows.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-30"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (Wisconsin DNR 2019 lapsed-hunter survey). Crossbows didn't drive gun hunters out: archery-season buck kills were a contributing factor for about 4-5% of lapsed gun hunters, and more lapsed hunters felt positive about crossbows (40%) than negative (18%). The DNR concludes crossbows are not a major contributor to the decline.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-31"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"MEASURED, one season, no counterfactual (Pennsylvania Game Commission data via CapitolWire/State Affairs in the Altoona Mirror, Jan 2026). Pennsylvania ended its Sunday-hunting ban (Act 36, 2025), yet adult resident licenses still fell 2.13% in 2025 (512,505 to 501,591). Out-of-state adults rose 3.2% and juniors were flat. The Commission's spokesman says the drop \"might have been worse\" without Sundays. That's its belief, not a measurement.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-32"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"DESCRIBED (a FORECAST, never checked). The Congressional Sportsmen's Foundation's 2011 study predicted 117,500 more hunters by 2016 if six strict states (CT, DE, ME, MA, PA, VA) eased Sunday bans. CSF is an advocacy group, and no follow-up comparing that forecast to actual sales was found. Pennsylvania's first full-Sunday year (T2-32) still lost hunters.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-33"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED by Southwick Associates for Florida FWC (Southwick's own summary; the full report's host is down). Florida's auto-renew option (Oct 2015) had no statistically significant effect on HUNTING license sales (under 1%), though fishing renewals rose 3.7%. No Michigan or Wisconsin auto-renew study was found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-34"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"GOLD SIDE, builds on C34. When Illinois ended sharpshooting, its own CWD report called the change a move to \"dependence upon increased hunter harvest\", effect unknown. But hunters in the 28 CWD counties took 30,926 deer in 2025-26, about what they took (31,037) in 2001-02, the last season before CWD was found. Leaning on hunters means leaning on a harvest that hasn't grown in 24 years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-35"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"GOLD SIDE. The same Illinois report lists what paid shooters could do that hunters can't: shoot only where the disease is, work on land that doesn't allow hunting (deer refuges), and remove sick animals at a higher rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-36"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"GOLD SIDE, A35/A36 follow-up. As of mid-2026 the Recovering America's Wildlife Act still isn't law, and lawmakers haven't agreed how to pay for it (Wikipedia, citing Vox, July 2026). Secondary. A 119th-Congress bill number is still not established (congress.gov blocked the reader), so A36's on-air rule stands: say \"it has never passed the Senate\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-37"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Does any Michigan or Wisconsin programme have a measured RETENTION effect on deer hunters with a comparison group? None was found. The only matched-comparison study (T2-1 to T2-5) covers eight other states. Wisconsin's adult Hunt for Food numbers (T2-11) have no comparison group, and Michigan's apprentice report (T2-20) was not found.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-38"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"EVALUATED (WMI). Practical corollary for the script: the authors' own advice is to screen events toward people new to hunting, because much of today's audience is already in.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-39"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The reframe as handed to the writer, and hand pass A's gloss on A22: \"half of each state's Pittman-Robertson share is apportioned by paid license holders\", so \"fewer license buyers does shrink a state's share\". Re-read at the statute: (1) the license-holder half applies to the REMAINDER after a separate slice (half of the tax on pistols, revolvers, bows and arrows) that is apportioned by population, so it is half of the main pot, not half of every dollar; (2) it is a RATIO to all states' license holders, counted in the second fiscal year back, so a state loses money only when its license holders fall faster than other states'. The FY2026 table's \"basic\" column ($679.3M of $842.4M, A20) is our reading of that remainder.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w1"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The Blue Book's Argument For: license sales are \"one-third of CPW's current annual revenue, about $115 million\" (D5). Can CPW's own page confirm it? No: CPW reports \"Licenses, Passes, Fees and Permits\" as one line (68% of $220M wildlife revenue; 58% of $378M agency-wide, A17), lumping hunting and fishing licenses with state-park passes and fees. The page can neither confirm nor refute $115M. It stays the supporters' figure, attributed.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-w2"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Light line: the Michigan DNR's deer specialist (Stewart) compared limiting bait sites to \"not hosting a dinner party when you're sick\", in the Feb 27, 2020 bulletin that is the source of D33 and D35.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w3"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Light line: at Oak Ridge (B13), most deer-vehicle collisions came at the start of the workday.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w4"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"Light line: the University of Washington's cougar release calls the deer \"North America's most dangerous mammal to humans\". A university press office's framing, not a finding; its own figures in the next sentence (1.2 million collisions, $1.66 billion) are not checked and do not air.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-w5"},{"s":"ep51-do-we-need-deer-hunters","n":51,"c":"The cold-open line in the rescope notes (2026-11-01): \"and there are fewer of them every year\". Re-read against A10 and A12 for the rewritten brief: Michigan's deer-license buyers ROSE 1.6% in 2024, and Wisconsin's deer hunter participation increased slightly in 2024. The decline is decades-long (Michigan −31% since 1995, A10; Wisconsin about −80,000 from 2005 to 2022, A11), not every year. Say \"fewer than a generation ago\" with the state on each number.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-w6"}]);